  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts for the Plinko board. It typechecks, but I couldn't run `vite build` or open it in a browser, so I haven't seen the rendering, the label alignment, or the physics behave.\n\n- **Scene:** an 8×12 board with a back panel, side walls and a base, and an open front. Metal pegs sit in 8 staggered rows of 7 and 6. Seven gold dividers separate seven bins with coloured floors and back strips. A warm key light, a blue rim light, an environment light and a hemisphere fill light it. The camera looks straight at the board with the whole thing in frame.\n- **Physics:** I wrote my own 2D simulation instead of using the engine's physics. It uses a fixed 1/240 s step, gravity 9.81, restitution 0.4, and collision impulses for ball–peg, ball–divider, ball–wall and ball–ball. The 20 balls spawn every 0.25 simulated seconds from top centre, with the offset `((i*37)%11-5)*0.02`. A small deterministic sideways nudge frees any ball that stalls balanced on a peg.\n- **Balls:** each ball is an Aura3D sphere with a runtime id. The simulation moves it each frame, and unspawned balls sit off-screen.\n- **UI:** the page has \"Drop 20 Balls\" and \"Reset\" buttons, a status readout, and the bin numbers 1 to 7 with a live count under each bin. These are HTML elements laid over the canvas. Their positions come from a vertical-fov projection I calculated, so they may not line up exactly with the bins.\n- **State and ready flag:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is a read-only getter that returns `{phase, spawned, bins[7], total}`, updated every frame. `phase` becomes `settled` once all 20 balls are in bins and have stopped. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()` resolves.\n- **Drop after settling:** clicking Drop after the balls have settled resets the board and starts a new drop.\n- **Build:** I avoided top-level await because the project has no vite config and the default build target would reject it.",
